 "title": "LAPLACE'S DEMON",
 "angle": "If a mind knew every particle in the universe, could it predict what you'll do next?",
 "answer": "Laplace said yes in 1814, but chaos makes the tiniest measurement error explode (Lorenz's rounding), quantum uncertainty forbids perfect measurement, and a 2008 proof shows no predictor inside the universe can predict everything, itself included; the future may be fixed, but it can never be fully read from inside.",
 "logline": "In 1814 Laplace imagined a mind that foresees everything, so is your future written? He said an intellect knowing every particle would see the future like the past, but in 1961 a rounded weather simulation diverged completely, so tiny errors explode, but the demon would need perfect data, and Heisenberg showed physics forbids it, but the strangest limit is logical: no predictor can predict itself, so the future may be fixed but no mind inside it can read it all.",
 "hook_text": "A MIND THAT KNOWS EVERYTHING",
 "hooks": [
  "Rounding one number from six decimals to three changed a whole simulated sky.",
  "In 2008, a physicist proved no machine can predict everything."
 ],
 "world": {
  "scenery": "default"
 },
 "script": [
  {
   "text": "In 1814, a mathematician imagined a mind that could foresee everything.",
   "say": "In eighteen fourteen, a mathematician imagined a mind that could foresee everything.",
   "beat": "hook"
  },
  {
   "text": "Including what you'll do next.",
   "beat": "hook",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "So if the universe runs like clockwork, is your future already written?",
   "beat": "question",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Pierre-Simon Laplace wrote that a mind knowing every particle and force would see the future like the past.",
   "say": "Pierre-Simon La-plass wrote that a mind knowing every particle and force would see the future like the past.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "answer"
  },
  {
   "text": "Later writers called it Laplace's demon.",
   "say": "Later writers called it La-plass's demon.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Asked by Napoleon where God fitted in, he reportedly said: I had no need of that hypothesis.",
   "beat": "reveal1",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "But in 1961, a weather simulation broke the dream.",
   "say": "But in nineteen sixty-one, a weather simulation broke the dream.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "To save time, a meteorologist re-ran it with numbers rounded from six decimals to three.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "detail"
  },
  {
   "text": "Within simulated weeks, the weather was completely different.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "Tiny errors explode: the famous butterfly in Brazil that sets off a tornado in Texas.",
   "beat": "reveal2",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the demon would also need perfect measurements, and physics forbids them.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "In 1927, Werner Heisenberg showed you can't know a particle's position and momentum exactly at once.",
   "say": "In nineteen twenty-seven, Werner Heisenberg showed you can't know a particle's position and momentum exactly at once.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "So even a perfect mind would start from a blur.",
   "beat": "reveal3",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "But the strangest limit is pure logic: in 2008, a physicist proved no predictor can predict everything.",
   "say": "But the strangest limit is pure logic: in two thousand and eight, a physicist proved no predictor can predict everything.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "but"
  },
  {
   "text": "Inside the universe, it would have to predict its own predictions, forever.",
   "beat": "turn",
   "link": "because"
  },
  {
   "text": "So the future might be fixed, but no mind inside it can ever read all of it.",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  },
  {
   "text": "If no one could ever predict your choice, would it matter whether it was written?",
   "beat": "payoff",
   "link": "so"
  }
 ],
